Inka's admin registers only two blocks itself, `slate` and `listing`; every other block a site uses is the frontend's to declare, with its `title`, `group` and `blockSchema`. The admin had its own configs for title, description, image, teaser, video, maps, html, table, the listing item types `default` and `summary`, search and its facets, and Volto's other blocks, and a frontend's entry was merged over them key by key: a key the frontend didn't send kept the admin's. So a frontend that drew an image with no link kept the admin image's mappings onto `href`, and a teaser it never offered kept a conversion reading an image's link, and the editor's load check refused both. A frontend that used any of these without declaring it must now declare it: the title and description blocks need their schema (the page field and its placeholder; see "Title and description blocks" in the custom-blocks guide), and search facets their conversion mappings.
